  "worked_solution": "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,2],$ the function must be continuous on $[0,2]$ and differentiable on $(0,2).$\n\nFirst, notice that the function $m(x)$ is continuous on $[0,2]$ and differentiable on $(0,2)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nLet's check each of the other functions individually.\n\n- The function $n(x)$  has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[0,2].$ Therefore, $n(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $l(x)$  has jump discontinuities at $x=0,2.$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[0,2].$ Therefore, $l(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $p(x)$  has a jump discontinuity at $x=2.$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[0,2].$ Therefore, $p(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $q(x)$  has removable discontinuities at $x=0,2.$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[0,2].$ Therefore, $q(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
